## 8. Scope – where variables live

> **In plain words:** a variable made **inside** a function exists **only inside that function** and disappears when the function ends. A variable made **outside** every function can be seen **everywhere**.

```js
let db = 0;              // GLOBAL: every function can see and change it

function tippel() {
  let t = 5;             // LOCAL: only exists inside tippel()
  db = db + 1;           // ✅ can use the global db
}

function kiir() {
  alert(db);             // ✅ works
  alert(t);              // ❌ ERROR: t is not defined (it belonged to tippel)
}
```

### Why this matters in the guessing game

```js
// ✅ GOOD – titok is outside, so indit() and tippel() share it
let titok;

function indit()  { titok = Math.floor(Math.random() * 100) + 1; }
function tippel() { if (t < titok) ... }
```

```js
// ❌ BAD – titok is created INSIDE indit(), tippel() can't see it
function indit()  { let titok = Math.floor(Math.random() * 100) + 1; }
function tippel() { if (t < titok) ... }   // error / undefined
```

> 💡 Rule: if **two or more functions** need the same value (secret number, counter, current ruler number) → declare it **outside**, at the top of the script.

### Same name inside and outside

```js
let x = 10;
function f() {
  let x = 99;     // a NEW local x, the global one is untouched
  alert(x);       // 99
}
f();
alert(x);         // 10
```

> ⚠️ Writing `let` again inside the function makes a NEW local variable. If you want to change the global one, **don't** write `let`: just `x = 99;`

### Parameters are local too

```js
function duplaz(szam) {
  szam = szam * 2;     // changes only the local copy
  return szam;
}
let a = 5;
duplaz(a);             // returns 10
alert(a);              // still 5
```

