## 13. Functions + HTML events

### Way 1 – In the HTML attribute (what your class uses)

```html
<button onclick="tippel()">Tippel</button>
<input id="szoveg2" onkeyup="masol()">
<img src="vonalzo.png" onmousemove="mozog(event)">

<script>
function tippel() { ... }
function masol()  { ... }
function mozog(event) {
  alert(event.offsetX);    // event = info about the mouse/key (position, key name...)
}
</script>
```

> ⚠️ In HTML you **call** it: `onclick="tippel()"`, **with** brackets.
> ⚠️ For mouse/key info, pass the word `event` as an argument: `onmousemove="mozog(event)"`. The function receives it as a parameter.

### Way 2 – From JavaScript (onclick property)

```js
document.getElementById("gomb").onclick = tippel;        // NO brackets! (give the function, don't run it)
document.getElementById("gomb").onclick = function () {  // or an anonymous function
  alert("Klikk!");
};
```

### Way 3 – addEventListener

```js
document.getElementById("gomb").addEventListener("click", tippel);   // NO brackets!

document.getElementById("kep").addEventListener("mousemove", function (event) {
  console.log(event.offsetX, event.offsetY);
});

document.addEventListener("keydown", (event) => {
  if (event.key == "Enter") tippel();
});
```

> ⚠️ **The most common bug:** `addEventListener("click", tippel())`. With `()` it runs **immediately** when the page loads, and gives its RESULT (undefined) to the button. Without `()` you hand over the function itself, so it runs **later, on click**.

| Event name in HTML | Event name in addEventListener |
|---|---|
| `onclick` | `"click"` |
| `onmousemove` | `"mousemove"` |
| `onkeyup` | `"keyup"` |
| `onchange` | `"change"` |

(Drop the `on` in addEventListener.)

